\description{
Reads the value of a FactorsForm model-terms option, with or without advanced term specification
(term definitions shared by the models, terms that combine definitions, restrictions), and gives the formula or the
numeric design columns of each model.

\code{prepareModelTerms()} validates the option against the fitting data and learns every data-dependent constant once.
\code{modelTermsFormula()} and \code{modelTermsDesign()} build the same model in formula mode and in data mode.
\code{modelTermsLabels()} translates the coefficient names of an \link[stats:lm]{stats::lm} or \link[stats:glm]{stats::glm} fit of the formula into
display labels. \code{modelTermsOffset()} gives the offset of a model for engines that take a design matrix, and
\code{modelTermsConstantOffset()} the constant part of it, which a formula cannot hold.
}
\details{
The formula of \code{modelTermsFormula()} has the calling environment as its environment (as
\link[stats:formula]{stats::as.formula}); its terms call only base, stats and splines functions.

Without advanced specification (no model has \code{definitions}) the formula is exactly
\code{response ~ a + b + a:b} in the order of \code{components}, with an intercept, factors are coded as R codes them,
and nothing is learned or checked. The ids of the terms are \code{t1}, \code{t2}, ... in the order R gives the terms of the
formula.

With advanced specification, a model has \code{definitions} and \code{terms}. A definition (\code{id}, \code{variable},
\code{specification}, the parameters of the specification and \code{restriction}) is one version of a variable, such as
\code{log(x)} or the treatment coding of a factor; the same definition (the same \code{id}) can be used by several models.
A term (\code{id}, \code{operands}) is the product of the versions named by its operands, the ids of definitions of the
model: one operand is a main effect, several are an interaction. The model has the terms in the order of \code{terms}.
A categorical version is the coded matrix of the factor, so an interaction is always the product of the coded
columns of its operands, also when a margin is missing. The terms come from \code{terms}, \code{components} is not read; a
model without \code{definitions} may not have \code{components} when another model has \code{definitions}. Definitions that no
term uses are ignored. Every inconsistency (an operand that is not a definition of the model, two terms with the
same operands, a definition without variable or specification, an unknown specification, two definitions with the
same settings, two codings of one nominal variable, a restriction with the wrong number of values) stops with a
message that names the model by its title. So does a value outside the domain of a specification, in the fitting
data and in new data.

A model with advanced specification may also have \code{intercept} (\code{TRUE} when absent) and, when the analysis allows
restrictions, \code{interceptRestriction} (\code{""} estimates the intercept freely, a numeric expression fixes it; it is
not read for a model without intercept).

The specifications, their parameters, the defaults of parameters a definition does not give and the labels of the
terms are listed in \code{modelTermSpecifications.json} of this package (the file jasp-desktop builds the form from). Rank
and normal scores depend on the whole sample, as \code{rank(x)} in a formula does: they are computed on the data the
formula is evaluated on, so \code{modelTermsDesign()} and \code{modelTermsOffset()} refuse new data for a model with one
(\code{sampleDependent} of the model); \code{predict()} on a fit of the formula does not know this, so a caller that predicts
checks \code{sampleDependent}. Indicator coding (one column per level) needs a model without intercept.

A restriction is one number or numeric expression per column of the term, separated by commas or semicolons;
\code{""} estimates the term freely. The restriction of a definition applies to the main effect of that definition (a
term with one operand); interaction terms are estimated freely. The intercept is one more term in \code{nested}: it is
free, fixed at a value, or (without intercept) fixed at 0. \code{modelTermsFormula()} does not enforce restrictions: the
caller fixes the coefficients, e.g. with the \code{fixed} attribute of \code{modelTermsDesign()}.

An offset adds a term to the linear predictor with its coefficient fixed at 1. There are two kinds. The specification
\code{offset} (continuous variables only, no parameters) is the offset of one variable: its values are the variable itself.
The specification \code{offsetConstant} has no variable but \code{value}, a finite number. An offset is a main effect that has no
coefficient: an offset of a variable gives \code{offset(x)} in the formula (so that \link[stats:lm]{stats::lm} and \link[stats:glm]{stats::glm} apply it),
a constant offset is not in the formula (they cannot take a constant offset there; \code{modelTermsConstantOffset()} gives
it, to be passed as \code{offset = rep(constant, nrow(data))}), and neither has a column in the design. \code{modelTermsOffset()}
has the values of all offsets. Several offsets add up. An offset cannot be an operand of an interaction and cannot
have a restriction. A nominal variable cannot be an offset, and a value that is not a finite number stops the
analysis. In \code{terms} an offset is a row without columns (\code{offset} is \code{TRUE}, \code{label} is \code{offset(x)} or
\code{offset(2)}), listed after the other terms. It counts as a term in \code{nested}, so models are nested only when they
have the same offsets (the same variables and the same constant values).
}
